WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.720
Deep learning has enabled us to&nbsp;build detailed specialized AI models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:10.929
and we can do that provided we gather enough data,

00:00:10.929 --> 00:00:14.506
label it, and use&nbsp;that to train and deploy those models.

00:00:14.506 --> 00:00:18.329
Models like customer service chatbots or fraud detection in&nbsp;banking.

00:00:18.758 --> 00:00:22.952
Now, in the past if you wanted to build a new model for your specialization -

00:00:22.952 --> 00:00:26.130
so, say a model for&nbsp;predictive maintenance in manufacturing -

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:30.193
well, you’d need to start again with data selection&nbsp;and curation,

00:00:30.193 --> 00:00:32.932
labeling, model development,&nbsp;training, and validation.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.216
But foundation models&nbsp;are changing that paradigm.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:42.792
So what is a foundation model?

00:00:42.886 --> 00:00:50.021
A foundation model is a more focused, centralized effort to create&nbsp;a base model.

00:00:50.021 --> 00:00:55.746
And, through fine tuning, that base foundation model can be adapted to a specialized&nbsp;model.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.347
Need an AI model for programming language translation?

00:00:59.347 --> 00:01:01.479
Well, start with a foundational model

00:01:01.479 --> 00:01:04.080
and&nbsp;then fine tune it with programming language data.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:10.140
Fine tuning and adapting base foundation&nbsp;models rapidly speeds up AI model development.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:12.616
So, how do we do that?

00:01:12.683 --> 00:01:17.464
Let’s look at the five&nbsp;stages of the workflow to create an AI model.

00:01:17.531 --> 00:01:24.194
Stage 1 is to prepare the data.

00:01:24.301 --> 00:01:30.322
Now in this stage we need to train&nbsp;our AI model with the data we're going to use,

00:01:30.322 --> 00:01:32.411
and we're going to need a lot of data.

00:01:32.411 --> 00:01:36.583
Potentially petabytes of data across&nbsp;dozens of domains.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:41.633
The data can combine both available open source data and proprietary&nbsp;data.

00:01:41.713 --> 00:01:48.679
Now this stage performs a series of data processing tasks.

00:01:48.773 --> 00:01:53.233
Those include&nbsp;categorization which describes what the data is.

00:01:53.233 --> 00:01:55.655
So which data is English, which is German?

00:01:55.655 --> 00:01:58.664
Which&nbsp;is Ansible which is Java? That sort of thing.

00:01:58.744 --> 00:02:02.880
Then the data is also applied with a filtere.

00:02:03.598 --> 00:02:08.041
So filtering allows us to, for example, apply filters for hate speech,

00:02:08.041 --> 00:02:10.224
and profanity and abuse, and that sort of thing.

00:02:10.452 --> 00:02:15.158
Stuff we want to filter out of the system that we don't train the model on it.

00:02:15.238 --> 00:02:20.215
Other filters may flag copyrighted material, private or sensitive information.

00:02:20.791 --> 00:02:25.550
Something else we're going to take out is  duplicate data as well.

00:02:25.831 --> 00:02:28.532
So we're going to remove that from there.

00:02:28.532 --> 00:02:35.798
And then that leaves us with something called a base data pile.

00:02:35.892 --> 00:02:39.781
So that's really the output of stage one.

00:02:39.781 --> 00:02:43.195
And this base data pile can be versioned and tagged.

00:02:43.195 --> 00:02:50.635
And&nbsp;that allows us to say, "This is what I’m training the AI model on, and here are the&nbsp;filters I used".

00:02:50.635 --> 00:02:52.081
It's perfect for governance.

00:02:52.584 --> 00:02:57.895
Now, Stage 2 is to train the model.

00:02:58.002 --> 00:03:01.505
And we're going to train the model on those base data piles.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.428
So we start&nbsp;this stage by picking the foundational model we want to use.

00:03:06.428 --> 00:03:09.572
So we will select our model.

00:03:10.443 --> 00:03:14.067
Now, there are many types of foundation models.

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:19.294
There are generative foundation&nbsp;models, encoder-only models, lightweight models, high parameter models.

00:03:19.549 --> 00:03:23.821
Are you looking to build an&nbsp;AI model to use as a chatbot, or as a classifier?

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.765
So pick the foundational model that matches your use&nbsp;case,

00:03:26.765 --> 00:03:30.248
then match the data pile with that model.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:36.892
Next we take the data pile and we tokenize it.

00:03:37.481 --> 00:03:44.760
Foundation models work with tokens rather than words, and a data pile could result&nbsp;in potentially trillions of tokens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:51.503
And now we can engage the process of training using all of those tokens.

00:03:51.503 --> 00:03:55.608
This process can take a long time, depending on the size of the model.

00:03:55.675 --> 00:04:00.462
Large scale foundation&nbsp;models can take months with many thousands of GPUs.

00:04:00.462 --> 00:04:05.280
But, once it’s done, the longest and&nbsp;highest computational costs are behind us.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.184
Stage 3 is "validate".

00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:13.798
When training is&nbsp;finished we benchmark the model.

00:04:13.798 --> 00:04:15.178
And this involves running the model

00:04:15.178 --> 00:04:18.285
and assessing its&nbsp;performance against a set of benchmarks

00:04:18.285 --> 00:04:20.701
that help define the quality of the model.

00:04:20.942 --> 00:04:25.596
And then from here we can create a model card

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.972
that says this is the model I’ve trained

00:04:28.972 --> 00:04:32.108
and&nbsp;these are the benchmark scores it has achieved.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:37.177
Now up until this point the main persona that&nbsp;has performed these tasks

00:04:37.177 --> 00:04:39.000
is the data scientist.

00:04:39.255 --> 00:04:42.390
Now Stage 4 is "tune",

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:46.871
and this is where we bring in the&nbsp;persona of the application developer.

00:04:46.871 --> 00:04:49.794
This persona does not need to be an AI expert.

00:04:49.794 --> 00:04:55.753
They engage with&nbsp;the model, generating - for example - prompts that elicit good performance from the model.

00:04:55.941 --> 00:05:02.852
They&nbsp;can provide additional local data to fine tune the model

00:05:02.852 --> 00:05:04.606
to improve its performance.

00:05:04.606 --> 00:05:08.357
And this stage is something that you can do in hours or days -

00:05:08.357 --> 00:05:11.788
much quicker&nbsp;than building a model from scratch.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:18.832
And now we’re ready for Stage 5, which is to deployment the model.

00:05:19.033 --> 00:05:24.426
Now this&nbsp;model could run as as service offering deployed to a public cloud.

00:05:24.841 --> 00:05:32.962
Or we could, alternatively, embed the model into an&nbsp;application that runs much closer to the edge&nbsp;of the network.

00:05:33.096 --> 00:05:38.639
Either way we can continue&nbsp;to iterate and improve the model over time.

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:45.426
Now here at IBM we’ve announced a platform&nbsp;that enables all 5 of the stages of this workflow.

00:05:45.426 --> 00:05:49.932
And It’s called watsonx and it’s composed of&nbsp;three elements.

00:05:49.932 --> 00:05:59.032
So we have: watsonx.data, watsonx.governance, and watsonx.ai.,

00:05:59.608 --> 00:06:07.740
and this all built on IBM’s&nbsp;hybrid cloud platform which is Red Hat OpenShift.

00:06:10.968 --> 00:06:15.737
Now Watsonx.data is a modern data lakehouse

00:06:15.737 --> 00:06:20.631
and establishes connections with the data&nbsp;repositories that make up the data in&nbsp;Stage 1.

00:06:20.819 --> 00:06:26.799
Watsonx.governance manages the data cards from Stage 1 and model cards from&nbsp;Stage 3

00:06:26.799 --> 00:06:31.807
enabling a collection of fact sheets that ensure a well-governed AI process and&nbsp;lifecycle.

00:06:31.807 --> 00:06:39.720
And watsonx.ai provides a means for the application developer persona&nbsp;to engage with the model in Stage 4.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:45.420
Overall foundation models are changing&nbsp;the way we build specialized AI models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:51.227
and this 5-stage workflow allows teams to&nbsp;create AI and AI-derived applications

00:06:51.227 --> 00:06:55.948
with greater sophistication while rapidly&nbsp;speeding up AI model development.

